 Actuator force alone, however, does not determine the torque a joint produces. The robot knee transmits BPA force to the skeleton through mounting brackets, artificial tendons, and paths that wrap over the joint, and each of these elements departs from the rigid-body idealization: brackets and fixtures deflect under load, tendons stretch, and a wrapping actuator loses usable length as the knee flexes. At the start of this work it was unknown how much these transmission effects reduce the torque delivered at the knee relative to the rigid-body prediction, and no method existed for correcting that prediction in a form that would transfer between joint configurations. The biomimetic knee's optimized muscle routing \citep{morrow_optimization_2020} compounded the problem: it had never been validated experimentally or compared against human isometric torque data, so even the nominal torque envelope of the robot leg rested on unverified calculations.
 
-There are additional gaps concerning the development of more detailed neural control models of locomotion and balance. No simulation pipeline existed for closing the loop between a musculoskeletal robot model of this kind and a biologically grounded neural controller. The neuromechanical modeling literature provides the ingredients, including two-level spinal central pattern generator architectures \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008}, neuromechanical simulation frameworks \citep{markin_afferent_2010}, and synthetic nervous system design tools \citep{nourse_sns_2023}, but it does not provide their assembly for a BPA-driven bipedal robot with human-like muscle routing. 
+The remaining gaps concern the controller. No simulation pipeline existed for closing the loop between a musculoskeletal robot model of this kind and a biologically realistic neural controller. The neuromechanical modeling literature provides the ingredients, including two-level spinal central pattern generator architectures \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008}, neuromechanical simulation frameworks \citep{markin_afferent_2010}, and synthetic nervous system tooling \citep{nourse_sns_2023}, but it does not provide their assembly for a BPA-driven bipedal robot with human-like muscle routing. Wiring realistic feedback into such a controller exposes a fourth gap, this one in the literature rather than in methods: the physiological properties of the group Ia, group Ib, and group II afferents that should inform the feedback design are scattered across hundreds if not thousands of primary papers and reviews \citep{van_de_crommert_review_1998, windhorst_muscle_2007, windhorst_spinal_2022}, with no structured resource from which a robot designer or an animal researcher could draw them.
 
