 Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} develops and validates the actuator force models. It presents the isometric force characterization of Festo BPAs with \qtylist{10;20}{\mm} uninflated diameters over a wide range of resting lengths, contractions, and pressures; derives improved maximum-force and normalized force--strain--pressure models; and compares them against the manufacturer's tool and published models. Later sections examine the effects of resting length, the implications of the validated models for real-time control and simulation, and the biomimetic analogy between braided pneumatic actuators and skeletal muscle. The chapter's text and figures are adapted from the author's publication of this work \citep{bolen_2026}.
 
-Chapter~\ref{ch:torque} improves the actuator model at the joint. It describes the robot leg model, the pinned-joint and biomimetic knee test stands, and the equipment used to collect isometric torque data; formulates the hybrid torque calculation method and the improved torque model whose correction terms account for a constant length offset, series compliance, and actuator wrapping; and documents the optimization algorithms used both to identify those terms and to redesign actuator routes. The chapter then reports the measured torque on both knees, discusses the physical sources of prediction error, presents the follow-up identification and the redesigned routes evaluated on the Gait2392 robot-body model, and compares the resulting torque envelopes with human capabilities, before closing on the transmission-design implications of mono- and bi-pulley routing.
+Chapter~\ref{ch:torque} carries the actuator models to the joint. It describes the robot leg model, the pinned-joint and biomimetic knee test stands, and the equipment used to collect isometric torque data; formulates the hybrid torque calculation method and the improved torque model whose correction terms account for a constant length offset, series compliance, and actuator wrapping; and documents the optimization algorithms used both to identify those terms and to redesign actuator routes. The chapter then reports the measured torque on both knees, discusses the physical sources of prediction error, presents the follow-up identification and the redesigned routes evaluated on the Gait2392 robot-body model, and compares the resulting torque envelopes with human capabilities, before closing on the transmission-design implications of mono- and bi-pulley routing.
 
 Chapter~\ref{ch:database} presents the Sensory Afferent Database, the structured afferent resource built to answer the afferent-literature gap. It describes the audited corpus and its curation coverage, in which papers carry structured curation notes, afferent classifications, and notes translating each sensory pathway toward robot or simulation use; the packaging of the corpus with its citation graph as a self-contained laboratory tool; and the evaluation of AI-assisted literature triage for expanding the database, before closing by mapping the corpus onto the controller's sensory channels.
 
-Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} builds the tools for neuromechanical simulation, with a stated goal: a closed-loop environment in which a synthetic nervous system controls the experimentally characterized robot model and supports the lesion, stimulation, and sensory-deletion experiments that cannot be performed on living subjects. It opens with the preliminary AnimatLab bipedal walker results, whose hand-tuned, two-layer CPG produced suspended stepping, with neither weight-bearing walking nor any role for afferent feedback in weight-bearing motion demonstrated; that outcome set the verification-first approach the developed tools use. The tools themselves are the OpenSim-to-MuJoCo conversion of the robot's musculoskeletal model with its synthetic BPA knee, the Simulink SNS library built to prototype reflex topologies, the spiking-neuron mirrors, and the cross-platform verification protocol that ties the implementations together. Each tool is validated before the complete model is assembled. The stimulus and deletion experiments complete the validation process, and the chapter closes by discussing a five-stage training curriculum, the spiking-neuron mirrors, and the new tools the program leaves other researchers.
+Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} builds the tools for neuromechanical simulation, with a stated goal: a closed-loop environment in which a synthetic nervous system controls the experimentally characterized robot model and supports the lesion, stimulation, and sensory-deletion experiments that cannot be performed on living subjects. It opens with the preliminary AnimatLab bipedal walker results, whose hand-tuned two-layer CPG produced suspended stepping, with neither weight-bearing walking nor any role for afferent feedback in weight-bearing motion demonstrated; that outcome set the verification-first approach the tools follow. The tools themselves are the OpenSim-to-MuJoCo conversion of the robot's musculoskeletal model with its synthetic BPA knee, the Simulink SNS library built to prototype reflex topologies, the spiking-neuron mirrors, and the cross-platform verification protocol that ties the implementations together. Each tool is demonstrated on the fundamental exercises that establish what it does and does not show: cross-engine reproduction of the contact-driven rhythm core, headless re-runs of the archived AnimatLab reference models, the BPA-actuated walker in MuJoCo, a gait-validation sweep against a locomotion reference library, and a comparison of the production walker variants. The stimulus and deletion experiments complete the demonstrations, and the chapter closes by discussing the five-stage training curriculum, the spiking-neuron mirrors, and the new tools the program leaves other researchers.
 
